{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

At a point $P$ of the <smooth algebraic curve> $X$, the <local ring> $\mathcal O_{X,P}$ is a <discrete valuation ring>. Embed the <projective variety> $Y$ in $\mathbb P^N$. A <rational map of algebraic varieties> to $Y$ has homogeneous coordinates $[f_0:\cdots:f_N]$ in the <function field> $k(X)$, not all zero. Let $v_P$ be the local <valuation>, choose a <uniformizer> $u$, and set $m=\min_{f_i\ne0}v_P(f_i)$. The coordinates $u^{-m}f_i$ are regular at $P$, and at least one is a unit. On a sufficiently small <open subset> containing $P$, all these functions are regular and that coordinate stays nonzero. Their ratios therefore define a <morphism of algebraic varieties> into the corresponding affine chart of $\mathbb P^N$. Every homogeneous equation defining $Y$ vanishes on the original dense open, hence vanishes identically on this neighborhood. The local extension consequently lands in $Y$.

Two such extensions agree wherever the original <rational map of algebraic varieties> is defined. Their equalizer is closed, because $Y$ is a <separated variety>, and contains a dense open. Thus they agree throughout their overlap and glue. **The rational map has a unique extension to a morphism on all of $X$.** Projectivity is needed for the target; the source need not be projective.

For the <function field> correspondence, let $K/k$ be a finitely generated <field extension> of <transcendence degree> one. Choose a <transcendence basis> consisting of one element $t$. The extension $K/k(t)$ is finite: finitely many algebraic generators each have finite degree. Take the <integral closures> of $k[t]$ and $k[t^{-1}]$ in $K$. The finiteness assumption allowed in the paper makes both finite modules over their original coordinate rings. <Integral closure> commutes with localization, so these two rings agree over $k[t,t^{-1}]$ and glue to the <finite normalization model of a one-variable function field> $C_K\to\mathbb P^1$. This is a <finite morphism>, hence projective, and its <function field> is $K$. Its one-dimensional normal local rings are <discrete valuation rings>, so it is regular. Since the <algebraically closed field> $k$ is perfect, regularity here implies smoothness. Thus $C_K$ is a <smooth projective curve>.

Conversely the <function field> of a <smooth projective curve> is finitely generated over $k$ and has <transcendence degree> one. Two such curves with isomorphic <function fields> have mutually inverse <birational maps>. The extension argument just proved makes both maps morphisms; their composites equal the identity on a dense open, and hence everywhere by <separatedness>. This proves uniqueness of the smooth projective model. More precisely, pullback gives a contravariant correspondence between nonconstant morphisms of <smooth projective curves> and $k$-embeddings of their <function fields>. A field embedding defines the map on affine coordinates near the <generic point>, and the extension argument completes it. Constant morphisms are excluded from this correspondence because they do not induce embeddings of <function fields>.

Finally let $f:C\to D$ be nonconstant. Its image is dense, and pullback gives a finite extension $k(D)\subset k(C)$: both fields have <transcendence degree> one and the latter is finitely generated. Normalize $D$ in $k(C)$, applying the allowed finite-<integral closure> result on affine charts. This gives a <finite morphism> $C'\to D$ with $C'$ a <smooth projective curve> and <function field> $k(C)$. The identification of <function fields> gives an isomorphism $C\cong C'$ by the preceding paragraph. Its composite with $C'\to D$ equals $f$ at the <generic point>, hence equals $f$ everywhere. **Every nonconstant morphism of these curves is finite.**

= 2
{parent=Paper 80}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Write the <affine variety> as $X=\operatorname{Spec}A$. Its coordinate ring is <Noetherian>, and a <coherent sheaf> is $\mathcal F=\widetilde M$ for a finitely generated $A$-module $M$. We first justify the acyclic resolution used to compute its <sheaf cohomology>.

If $I$ is an <injective module>, then $\widetilde I$ is a <flasque sheaf>. Indeed every open $U$ in this <Noetherian scheme> has the form $X\setminus V(\mathfrak a)$ for a finitely generated ideal. Clearing denominators gives
$$
\Gamma(U,\widetilde I)=\varinjlim_n\operatorname{Hom}_A(\mathfrak a^n,I).
$$
Here a homomorphism $h:\mathfrak a^n\to I$ gives, on a principal open $D(f)$ with $f\in\mathfrak a$, the fraction $h(f^n)/f^n$; the fractions agree on overlaps. Conversely choose fractions for a section on a finite principal-open cover. A common power clears their denominators and the finitely many compatibility relations. Multiplication by a further power of $\mathfrak a$ kills any elements supported on $V(\mathfrak a)$, producing a well-defined map on some $\mathfrak a^n$. Changing that power accounts for the direct limit. This is the <sheafification of an injective module is flasque> construction. Since $I$ is injective, each map $\mathfrak a^n\to I$ extends to $A\to I$. Thus every section on every open extends to $X$. Extending globally and restricting again also proves surjectivity for restrictions between arbitrary opens.

Choose an <injective module> resolution $0\to M\to I^0\to I^1\to\cdots$. Localization is exact, so sheafifying gives a <flasque resolution> of $\mathcal F$. Its global-section complex is exactly $I^\bullet$, since $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec}A,\widetilde I)=I$. This complex is exact in positive degrees, and a <flasque resolution> computes <sheaf cohomology>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{H^i(X,\mathcal F)=0\quad(i\ge1).}
$$
The argument actually proves <vanishing of quasi-coherent cohomology on an affine scheme>, without finite generation of $M$.

For the intersection assertion, varieties are taken to be separated, as usual. The <diagonal morphism> $X\to X\times_kX$ is a <closed immersion>. Its inverse image over $U\times_kV$ identifies $U\cap V$ with a closed subvariety of the <affine variety> $U\times_kV$. A closed subvariety of an <affine variety> is affine: its coordinate ring is the ambient coordinate ring modulo its defining ideal. **Thus $U\cap V$ is affine.** Repeated application gives affine finite intersections. Without <separatedness> this statement need not hold.

Choose a finite affine <open cover> $(U_\alpha)$ of the <projective variety> $X$. For ordered indices write $U_{\alpha_0\cdots\alpha_p}=U_{\alpha_0}\cap\cdots\cap U_{\alpha_p}$. The answer is the cohomology of the <Čech cochain complex>
$$
C^p=\prod_{\alpha_0<\cdots<\alpha_p}\Gamma(U_{\alpha_0\cdots\alpha_p},\mathcal F),\qquad
(dc)_{\alpha_0\cdots\alpha_{p+1}}=
\sum_{j=0}^{p+1}(-1)^j c_{\alpha_0\cdots\widehat{\alpha_j}\cdots\alpha_{p+1}}|_{U_{\alpha_0\cdots\alpha_{p+1}}}.
$$
In particular $H^0=\ker(C^0\to C^1)$ and, in positive degree,
$$
\boxed{H^i(X,\mathcal F)\cong\ker(d:C^i\to C^{i+1})/\operatorname{im}(d:C^{i-1}\to C^i).}
$$
The chart modules $\Gamma(U_\alpha,\mathcal F)$, together with their restriction and gluing maps, supply the modules on overlaps by localization. The gluing maps are essential; abstract chart modules alone do not specify a <sheaf>.

For completeness, take a <flasque resolution> $\mathcal F\to\mathcal I^\bullet$ and its double complex of <Čech cochain complexes> on this cover. In the resolution direction, its cohomology is $H^q(U_{\alpha_0\cdots\alpha_p},\mathcal F)$, which vanishes for $q>0$ by the affine result. In the cover direction, the augmented <Čech cochain complex> of a <flasque sheaf> is exact: sections can be extended from overlaps, successively adjusting a cocycle to eliminate one cover member at a time. Equivalently the augmented sheaf complex is stalkwise contracted by inserting a cover index containing the point. Thus computing the total cohomology in the two directions identifies <Čech cohomology> with <sheaf cohomology>, proving the displayed description.

